# The reframe: we're calling this an acoustics problem. It's an information problem with an acoustic delivery mechanism.

**Virtual-Christian brief — Quiet-by-design standards for MWAA terminals**

## 1. The reframe

**A terminal isn't loud because it has too many announcements. It's loud because the noise map and the information map don't line up.** Where noise happens is supposed to tell a passenger where information is; when it stops doing that, the passenger tunes out the whole environment, and the announcement that actually matters — the gate change, the security recall, the evacuation — gets processed as background. Quiet-by-design, framed as an acoustics standard, treats decibels as the deliverable. That is the wrong deliverable. The deliverable is *legible signal*. If MWAA writes a spec that hits STI 0.50 at 1 kHz in every gate lounge and does nothing to govern *who has authority to trigger a global page and what it costs them to do so*, the terminals will still feel loud and the important messages will still be missed. The acoustic standard is necessary. It is not the argument. The argument is that "quiet" is a symptom of good information governance, not a design objective on its own.

That is the one sentence I would like the Strategist to steal: **quiet is a symptom of good information governance, not a design objective**.

## 2. Five connect-the-dots arguments

### 2.1 The ICU alarm-fatigue parallel — this is a governance problem in an acoustics costume.

Hospitals spent the 2000s discovering that between 86% and 99.5% of physiologic monitor alarms were false, and that clinicians had adapted by tuning them out — the phenomenon the FDA and Joint Commission eventually named "alarm fatigue." The Joint Commission response in 2014 was NPSG.06.01.01, and it is instructive that the standard is not "have fewer alarms." It is: leaders make alarm safety a hospital priority (EP1); identify the alarms that matter, informed by clinical input and history of harm (EP2); write policies for clinically appropriate settings and for when alarms may be disabled (EP3). It is a *governance* standard implemented through equipment. Airports are on the same trajectory, running the same play, ten years behind. The MWAA standard that will actually work is the one that answers "who can trigger which category of page, under what authority, with what audit trail, and with what escalation to a supervisor" — not the one that lists STI thresholds by zone. The ACRP 175 numbers are the easy part. The governance is the part that will hold up in a five-year post-occupancy review.

The corollary is the one every hospital CIO learned the hard way: **the moment you build a policy for "when a page is authorized," gate agents and airline ops centers immediately try to route around it.** So the standard also has to answer the question NPSG.06.01.01 answers in its subtext: who owns the exception, and what does an exception cost.

### 2.2 The public-library re-noise-ification cycle — silence was never the model.

Public libraries in the United States spent the 1990s enforcing silence as an operating norm, then spent the 2000s and 2010s discovering that silent libraries lost teenagers, working professionals, small business owners, community groups, and eventually foot traffic. The winning model that emerged — visible in Seattle Central, Salt Lake City Main, Chicago Harold Washington, and most academic learning-commons rebuilds — was not silence. It was *zoned, legible noise*: loud children's floors, buzzing cafes, quiet reading rooms, whisper-only stacks, telephone booths for calls, glassed-in group-study rooms. The user learns the noise language of the building in the first thirty seconds and self-selects. The direct analog for MWAA: a "quiet by design" standard that quiets the whole terminal will lose the airline gate agent, the international arrivals family group, the missed-connection scramble, and the group traveler who *needs* to hear the announcement because they cannot read the FIDS. Zone the noise. Let the concourse spine be a plaza, let the gate lounge be a library, let the international arrivals hall be a market, let the meditation room be a cathedral. Make the transition audible on purpose.

### 2.3 The CNN Airport Network shutdown — a natural experiment nobody analyzed.

On March 31, 2021, CNN killed CNN Airport, which had been broadcasting muted-cable-news audio and captioned video into 58 U.S. terminals for thirty years. In a single day, one of the largest continuous background-audio sources in American commercial aviation went dark. This is the closest thing the industry has had to a controlled trial in ambient-noise reduction, and to the best of my knowledge nobody has published a rigorous before/after on speech intelligibility, missed-boarding rates, or passenger complaint volume across those 58 airports. That is a research gap MWAA could exploit — a targeted post-hoc study across peer airports that lost CNN Airport at the same moment would give a defensible baseline for what happens when you remove *unmanaged* background audio without changing anything else. It would also give MWAA a cheap piece of evidence to shove in front of a skeptical airline before the standard hardens: if the sky did not fall at ATL, DFW, or ORD when CNN Airport left, the "we need the ambient noise for customer engagement" argument from tenants shrinks fast.

### 2.4 The Amazon-fulfillment-center analogy — the sleeper metric is employee turnover, not passenger NPS.

Occupational noise research from NIOSH, OSHA, and the operators-manual literature is unambiguous that sustained exposure above roughly 85 dBA drives measurable hearing loss and, more relevantly for a terminal argument, drives measurable attrition. NIOSH puts the workers-compensation cost of occupational hearing loss around $242 million per year. Amazon and other high-volume logistics operators spent a decade re-engineering pick-station noise environments precisely because ambient noise was a top-quartile predictor of associate quit rate. Terminals have the same problem: TSA officers spend 8-hour shifts in the loudest zone in the building, gate agents work 6-hour turns at podium mics under a hard-surfaced ceiling, and airline ramp coordinators are exposed to a stew of ground-support noise, PA overlap, and radio traffic. Every operator I have worked with knows that turnover at the frontline is the single largest hidden cost in the terminal. **The quiet-by-design business case that will actually get a "yes" from the CFO isn't the passenger-comfort case. It is the workers-comp, TSA-attrition, and airline-labor-productivity case, and the standard should quantify it as such.** Concessions get the customer-comfort headline; the finance committee gets the OpEx line.

### 2.5 The Saarinen constraint — the standard has to name its exception before publication, or it dies at the exception.

IAD's Main Terminal is a 1978 National Register of Historic Places building, designed by Eero Saarinen as a hard-surfaced ceremonial hall in which reverberation is a feature, not a defect. Any "quiet by design" standard that goes anywhere near it either (a) quietly exempts the Main Terminal — in which case it is not a common MWAA standard, it is a common-except-the-most-famous-space MWAA standard — or (b) triggers a Section 106 review, State Historic Preservation Office coordination, and probably a fight with the DC-area preservation community that will consume 12-18 months of program time. This is the boring but decisive detail. The standard must name its historic-property carveout on page 1 of Volume 1, with the exception governed by an operational compensating-control table (mobile audio kits, directional loudspeakers used only for irregular ops, visual-first digital signage with staffed roving supplements). If the standard leaves the Saarinen question open, the first Section 106 review will effectively write the standard for MWAA — and that is a bad way to lose control of your own design manual.

## 3. The operator's gut check

Here is what I would say at the bar, word for word:

> **"The reason your terminal is loud isn't the PA. It's the tile floor, the hard ceiling, the CNN feed nobody's watching, the airline gate agent yelling into a $40 handheld because the podium mic never worked right, the muted TVs at every seat cluster, the roller-bag wheels on porcelain, and the fact that Concourse D was built in 1985 for people who checked a bag and read a newspaper. You can spec STI 0.65 all day; you're still going to have a gate agent shouting 'Group 5!' because the boarding process is *designed* for chaos. Fix boarding, fix the floor material, fix who owns the podium mic, and you'll have solved 70% of the noise problem before anyone tunes the PA."**

Plain-language corollaries an operator would recognize:

- **The gate podium is where the standard lives or dies.** MWAA doesn't own the podium; the airline does. If the standard doesn't cover tenant/airline-installed audio equipment in the tenant use-and-lease and the tenant construction manual, it doesn't cover 60% of the noise generators in the building.
- **A "quiet" DCA cannot turn its aircraft.** DCA is slot-controlled, high-frequency, 25-30 minute turns, information changing four times per boarding cycle. It is loud because the operation is loud. If the standard tries to make DCA look like Copenhagen, the operation breaks. IAD international pier is the opposite: dwell times measured in hours, non-English-first traveler share above the national average, and the announcement is often the only signal the pax has that isn't printed in English.
- **The passenger complaint that will kill the program isn't "too loud." It's "I missed my flight because the announcement wasn't broadcast in my zone."** SFO is already getting some of this from the "why don't you page my name" cohort — the exact failure mode the standard needs to design against.
- **You will not commission this once.** PA systems drift; concessions change tenants; airlines rotate ground-handling contractors; new furniture arrives with hard surfaces. The standard has to require *recurring* commissioning — annually or per-tenant-change — or it decays into the same "wait, that wasn't in the spec" fight in year three.

## 4. Productive tangents

Things I went down that turned out to matter:

- **Alarm fatigue is bidirectional.** Too many pages, passengers tune out. Too few pages, passengers *also* tune out — they stop attending to the environment because they've been trained that it doesn't carry information. The right target is *legible signal density*, not minimum decibel level. This shows up in ATC readback research and in NICU alarm literature.
- **The tenant problem is the whole game.** The standard must reach airline gate podiums, airline club lounges, concessionaire storefront audio, muted-TV media systems, and rental-car counters. MWAA has the vehicle for this — the Design Manual, Specifications, use-and-lease riders — but the standard as drafted will be tempted to focus on MWAA-owned common space, which is the easy 40% of the noise.
- **The CNN Airport Network shutdown as unexploited natural experiment** (see 2.3). Small research spend, disproportionate evidentiary payoff.
- **The IAD Concourse E opening this fall (2026)** is the first real-world integration test for whatever standard MWAA lands on — 14 new United gates, direct AeroTrain access, new lounges. If the standard is not at least in draft form by the time E goes into ORAT, the standard misses the first opportunity to prove itself on a new building.
- **DCA post-collision messaging load.** After January 29, 2025, DCA's irregular-operations messaging burden went up — helicopter route changes, runway restrictions during helicopter ops, higher visibility on safety comms. Any "reduce announcements" framing has to survive the reality that DCA now has more mandatory safety pages, not fewer.

Two I chased that mostly did not pan out but I am leaving in:

- **Muzak history.** I went down a rabbit hole on the history of programmed music in public space — Muzak Holdings, the Boesch acoustic patents, the theory of "stimulus progression." Fun, and it explains why the "airport-jazz-piano-loop" thing feels the way it does, but it is not load-bearing for the recommendation. Leaving it in only because it is the historical answer to "why do public spaces have background audio in the first place?" and that question will come up.
- **Concert-hall acoustics (Sabine, McKim).** I looked into whether Boston Symphony Hall's Sabine coefficients had anything useful to say for a terminal. They don't, really — the design intent is opposite. But the exercise clarified for me that the "reverberation is bad" reflex in the quiet-airport literature is wrong; reverberation is a design variable, and in the Saarinen Main Terminal it is a deliberate one. Kept the tangent because it reframes how the Strategist should talk about the Main Terminal exception.

## 5. Evidence gaps I am flagging honestly

- Whether Copenhagen, Helsinki, or Amsterdam Schiphol have *quietly reversed* portions of their silent-airport programs — my operator instinct says at least some walkback has happened, especially for irregular-ops messaging, but I could not find primary-source confirmation in a targeted search. The Strategist should not assume the international-peer story is a linear "silent-and-happy" narrative until someone confirms it with primary evidence.
- Whether the SFO "40% noise reduction" figure is airport-reported or independently measured. The public reporting is uniformly one-sourced back to SFO. Treat the headline number as directionally right and methodologically unverified until an independent measurement surfaces.
- Whether MWAA's 2020 Design Manual already contains acoustic, PA-zoning, or STI-tier requirements. The Stage 0 packet flags this as unverified. If the manual already has these, the recommendation is amendment; if it does not, the recommendation is new-volume — and the political weight of the two is different.

